menu "Station: network"

    config NETWORK_AP_SSID_PREFIX
        string "Setup AP SSID prefix"
        default "onocoydiy-"
        help
            The setup access point is named <prefix><last 4 hex digits of MAC>.

    config NETWORK_AP_CHANNEL
        int "Setup AP channel"
        range 1 13
        default 1

    config NETWORK_STA_FALLBACK_S
        int "Seconds without Wi-Fi before the setup AP also comes up"
        range 30 3600
        default 300
        help
            The station keeps retrying its configured network; after this long
            without a connection it additionally starts the setup AP (AP+STA)
            so the user can fix the credentials. The AP stops again
            NETWORK_AP_LINGER_S after the station has an IP (so the setup page
            can still show the new address).

    config NETWORK_AP_LINGER_S
        int "Seconds the setup AP stays up after the station got its IP"
        range 10 120
        default 15
        help
            An AP that is up for first setup or as the offline fallback stays
            up this long once the station has an IP on its network, so the
            setup page (on a phone still on the AP) can show the station's
            new address and count down to the moment the AP closes. Then the
            phone goes back to its own Wi-Fi and finds the station there.
            Not used for the recovery AP (BOOT button), which has its own
            lifetime.

    config NETWORK_RECOVERY_AP_S
        int "Recovery AP lifetime after a BOOT-button press (s)"
        range 120 3600
        default 900
        help
            The AP started by the BOOT button (admin password cleared, or
            settings unreadable) goes down again after this long, or later
            when the last connected client leaves, but at most 30 min after
            this lifetime even while a client stays connected.

    config NETWORK_WIFI_COUNTRY
        string "Wi-Fi country code"
        default "01"
        help
            ISO 3166 alpha-2 code, or "01" for the conservative worldwide
            setting. Sets the allowed channels and transmit power.

    config NETWORK_FALLBACK_DNS1
        string "Fallback DNS server 1"
        default "1.1.1.1"
        help
            Used by the network recovery ladder when the DHCP-provided DNS
            stops answering.

    config NETWORK_FALLBACK_DNS2
        string "Fallback DNS server 2"
        default "9.9.9.9"
        help
            Second server for the same switch. Both are also the targets of
            the upstream probe's TCP check (port 53) when a name lookup
            fails, which tells a DNS failure from no internet at all.

    config NETWORK_PROBE_HOST
        string "Upstream probe host"
        default "github.com"
        help
            Name looked up and connected to (TCP, closed at once, nothing
            sent) by the upstream probe, which tells "no IP" from "IP but no
            upstream" for the network recovery ladder. The default is a host
            the station talks to anyway (update check). Never a caster: a
            bare connect could count as a failed source login there.

    config NETWORK_PROBE_PORT
        int "Upstream probe TCP port"
        range 1 65535
        default 443

    config NETWORK_ROAM
        bool "Move between the access points of one network (mesh)"
        depends on !NETWORK_QEMU_ETHERNET
        default y
        help
            For a network of several access points under one name (a mesh,
            or a router with repeaters). The station tells its access point
            that it takes 802.11k radio measurement and 802.11v BSS
            transition requests, so the network can steer it to a better
            node, and while its own signal is weak it looks for a clearly
            stronger access point of the network and reconnects to it.
            Every move drops the caster sessions for a few seconds, so moves
            are rare: none during a firmware update or a receiver flash,
            none while the station's own access point is up or changed
            credentials are on trial, at most one the network asks for per
            5 min (unless it disconnects the station anyway) and one of the
            station's own per NETWORK_ROAM_MIN_INTERVAL_S. A strong signal
            never makes the station scan. Each move goes to the event
            journal. Off: the station joins the strongest access point when
            it connects and stays there until the link drops.
            The 802.11k/v support itself is ESP_WIFI_11KV_SUPPORT (on in
            sdkconfig.defaults).

    config NETWORK_ROAM_WEAK_DBM
        int "Weak signal for the station's own check (dBm)"
        depends on NETWORK_ROAM
        range -90 -50
        default -70
        help
            The station looks for a stronger access point only while its
            signal, averaged over about 20 s, has been at or below this for
            a minute.

    config NETWORK_ROAM_MIN_GAIN_DB
        int "How much stronger another access point must be (dB)"
        depends on NETWORK_ROAM
        range 6 30
        default 12
        help
            An access point of the same network at least this much stronger
            than the current one, in two scans 30 s apart, makes the station
            reconnect (the connect joins the strongest access point).

    config NETWORK_ROAM_MIN_INTERVAL_S
        int "Shortest time between two of the station's own moves (s)"
        depends on NETWORK_ROAM
        range 300 86400
        default 1800
        help
            Counted from the previous move of either kind, so a move the
            access point asked for also stands for at least this long.

    config NETWORK_QEMU_ETHERNET
        bool "QEMU development build: emulated Ethernet instead of Wi-Fi"
        depends on ETH_USE_OPENETH
        default y
        help
            For running the firmware in Espressif's QEMU only (the overlay
            sdkconfig.qemu at the repository root, tools/qemu/run.sh). It
            can only be turned on together with the OpenCores Ethernet MAC
            driver (ETH_USE_OPENETH), which exists in QEMU and on no real
            chip, and never with the defaults a station is built from.

            Wi-Fi is never initialised or started (QEMU has no radio). The
            emulated Ethernet port takes the station interface's place:
            same interface key and IP events, link up and down reported as
            the station's association, DHCP from QEMU's user-mode network.
            There is no setup AP. The web server accepts the Host names
            localhost and 127.0.0.1 (QEMU's port forward) and treats every
            request as if it came over the setup AP, so the first admin
            password can be set from the development machine.

            Configure refuses a release build with this option, and the
            release job refuses to publish one.

endmenu
